  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e812(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 12(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9(1-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=\u27e81,6\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e81,6\u27e9 & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${C}_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+7\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\u27e8\\frac{3}{2}(1+2(1))^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3(1))^{3}+7\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8121,15\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
